; Devirtualization with the "types" resolver: the alias set of an indirect
; call is keyed on the callee signature, so the call in test-devirt-types.c is
; promoted to the two callees that share its signature and to nothing else.
;
; With an alias set keyed on the type of the called operand instead -- `ptr`
; for every indirect call under opaque pointers -- the call is also promoted
; to @two_args, and passing one argument to a two-parameter callee makes the
; first RUN below fail outright with "Callee is not a pointer type".
;
; RUN: %clam -O0 --crab-dom=int --devirt-functions=types --crab-print-invariants=false --crab-disable-warnings "%s".c -o %t.bc
; RUN: %llvm_dis < %t.bc | OutputCheck %s --comment=";"
; RUN: %llvm_dis < %t.bc | OutputCheck %s --check-prefix=NEG --comment=";"

; Both same-signature callees are called directly. The alias set is ordered by
; pointer address, so do not assume which one comes first.
; CHECK: define i32 @main
; CHECK: call i32 @add_(one|two)\(i32 noundef %call\)
; CHECK: call i32 @add_(one|two)\(i32 noundef %call\)

; And no other callee is. The volatile store of @two_args is the last mention
; of it that belongs in main, and it precedes the promoted call, so anything
; naming @two_args after it comes from an alias set that ignored signatures.
; NEG: store volatile ptr @two_args
; NEG-NOT: @two_args
